# The In-band RESET command. Hand built -- see below for why the capture could
# not supply this one.
#
# Section 8.3.2, pp.122-123, and Figure 65 on p.123. RESET is the only opcode
# in Table 2 with no CRC byte and no response phase, so both transactions here
# are a command phase and nothing else. There is no TAR line and there is no
# RSP line, and the decoder must agree: reading a CRC or turning around would
# leave the frame short and the test says so.
#
# Decoded in Single I/O, where a byte is eight clocks.
#
# 1. The command Figure 65 draws: sixteen eSPI clocks with every I/O line
#    driven high, which in Single I/O is two bytes. The first is the opcode
#    FFh; the second is bits the target ignores.
CMD FF FF

# 2. The same command with chip select deasserting straight after the opcode --
#    eight clocks, half of what Figure 65 draws, and nothing left for the target
#    to ignore.
#
#    NOTHING HERE IS TRUNCATED, and that is the point of the case. RESET
#    requires no byte after the opcode, so a frame that stops there is complete
#    in the only sense the decoder can check; it just does not match the figure.
#    Every other opcode in the table would report a missing CRC.
CMD FF

# WHY THIS IS NOT A CAPTURE FIXTURE. espi_dump.txt does open with a RESET, but
# it opens *inside* it: chip select is already asserted on the export's
# pre-trigger row, so the frame's byte boundaries are wherever the other decoder
# started counting and its twelve bytes are neither sixteen clocks nor all ones.
# That frame is worth decoding and it is decoded, in espi_dump.expected -- it is
# just not evidence about what a well formed RESET looks like.
